<Ui xmlns="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/ui/"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/FrameXML/UI.xsd">

	<Script file="SwitchesUI.lua" />

	<!--
		One switch. Its name, what it comes up as, what it is right now, and the menu.

		**The name is drawn with its `$`**, which is the one thing on this row that is not a
		setting: it is the text the user types into a macro body, so the list is where those glyphs
		get learned (§6-B of `devdocs/redesigning-custom-states.md`).

		Art matches the main list, the export list and the spell picker
		(`ClickCastList-ButtonBackground`). Four lists in one addon that look like four things is
		four things to learn.

		**The row is not a click target.** Every gesture on it belongs to a named control: the
		toggle to the button, the menu to the arrow. "Left-click toggles" on a row that also
		carries a delete is a misclick that costs a switch. Right-click anywhere opens the
		menu, which is the gesture the main list already teaches.
	-->
	<Button name="DebindSwitchRowTemplate" mixin="DebindSwitchRowMixin" virtual="true"
		registerForClicks="RightButtonUp">
		<Size x="400" y="28"/>
		<Layers>
			<Layer level="BACKGROUND">
				<Texture parentKey="Background" atlas="ClickCastList-ButtonBackground">
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/>
						<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT"/>
					</Anchors>
				</Texture>
			</Layer>
			<Layer level="ARTWORK">
				<!--
					**What the switch comes up as**, which is one of the four answers a switch can
					give (§4-6). Not the mode: "Set Manually" on its own does not say whether the
					switch comes up on, comes up off, or comes back the way it was left, and those
					are three different switches to live with.

					**This column is the fixed one and the name takes what is left**, which is the
					way round the export list already does it (`Key` there, declared before the
					name for this reason). The answers are four known strings and the widest of
					them is what 140 is measured against; a name is short in practice and is the
					one thing the row's tooltip repeats, so it is the safer of the two to clip.
					Sized the other way round, a `$state3` eight characters wide reserved 170 and
					left the answer to be cut into an ellipsis.

					**Its right edge is a number rather than the toggle's left edge.** A layer is
					laid out before the frames below are declared, so an anchor naming one of them
					reaches nothing and is dropped without a word. The string would end up with a
					single horizontal point, no width, and draw nothing at all. The number is the
					two buttons plus their gaps: 4 + 22 + 2 + 58 + 6.
				-->
				<FontString parentKey="Setting" inherits="GameFontDisableSmall" maxLines="1" justifyH="RIGHT">
					<Size x="140" y="0"/>
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="RIGHT" x="-92" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</FontString>
				<!--
					**Declared after the column it hangs off**, so the anchor finds it. Both
					horizontal points are set, which is what gives the name a width without a
					number of its own: it ends where the answer begins.
				-->
				<FontString parentKey="Name" inherits="GameFontNormal" maxLines="1" justifyH="LEFT">
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="LEFT" x="8" y="0"/>
						<Anchor point="RIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.Setting" relativePoint="LEFT" x="-6" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</FontString>
			</Layer>
			<Layer level="HIGHLIGHT">
				<Texture parentKey="FrameHighlight" atlas="ClickCastList-ButtonHighlight" setAllPoints="true" alphaMode="ADD">
					<Color r="1" g="1" b="0" a="1"/>
				</Texture>
			</Layer>
		</Layers>
		<Frames>
			<!--
				The menu, and the only place this row can be renamed or deleted from.

				**Visible rather than right-click only.** The main list can hide its menu behind
				right-click because every row there is something the user made and put a key on;
				a switch's whole configuration is in here, so a list with no visible handle is a
				list that looks like it does nothing.

				`UIPanelSquareButton` with the DOWN icon is the addon's own arrow
				(`DebindUI.xml`'s move buttons), which is why the icon is set in Lua.
			-->
			<Button parentKey="MenuButton" inherits="UIPanelSquareButton">
				<Size x="22" y="22"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="RIGHT" x="-4" y="0"/>
				</Anchors>
				<Scripts>
					<!-- No mixin on these two: the row owns the state, so the click goes there. -->
					<OnClick>
						self:GetParent():OnMenuClick();
					</OnClick>
				</Scripts>
			</Button>
			<!--
				**A plain button, deliberately.** Making it a secure one would put a protected
				frame inside the window, and the reason this window can stay open in combat is
				that it holds none (F-3 of `devdocs/redesigning-custom-states.md`). So it is
				disabled in combat, and turning a switch on mid-fight is the switch bar's job
				(§6-A) or a key carrying an on/off/toggle action.

				`motionScriptsWhileDisabled` so the tooltip can say why it is greyed.
			-->
			<Button parentKey="ToggleButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate"
				motionScriptsWhileDisabled="true">
				<Size x="58" y="20"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="RIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.MenuButton" relativePoint="LEFT" x="-2" y="0"/>
				</Anchors>
				<Scripts>
					<OnClick>
						self:GetParent():OnToggleClick();
					</OnClick>
					<OnEnter>
						self:GetParent():OnToggleEnter();
					</OnEnter>
					<OnLeave>
						GameTooltip:Hide();
					</OnLeave>
				</Scripts>
			</Button>
		</Frames>
		<Scripts>
			<OnLoad method="OnLoad"/>
			<OnClick method="OnClick"/>
			<OnEnter method="OnEnter"/>
			<OnLeave method="OnLeave"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Button>

	<!--
		**A panel, not a window.** It is the fourth seat in `PANELS` (`DebindUI.lua`), reached by
		the Switches tab. Everything the export panel's own header says about being a panel holds here
		word for word: no chrome, no ESC, no drag, no `enableMouse`, `useParentLevel`, and a
		`preferredWidth` the frame reads when the tab is chosen.

		**The rectangle is Overview's.** Left 4, top -85, right -31 are the three every tab shares so
		the list does not jump as you switch, and the bottom is the export panel's 50. That band is
		a button row, and since 3c this panel has one: making a switch. It said 10 here while making
		one was the portrait's dropdown, which is the door that closed.
	-->
	<Frame name="DebindSwitchesPanel" parent="DebindFrame" parentKey="SwitchesPanel" hidden="true"
		useParentLevel="true" setAllPoints="true" mixin="DebindSwitchesPanelMixin">
		<KeyValues>
			<!-- Overview's width. One list this narrow has nothing to spend more on, and keeping
			     the number means the window does not resize between these two tabs. -->
			<KeyValue key="preferredWidth" value="440" type="number"/>
		</KeyValues>
		<Frames>
			<Frame parentKey="ScrollBoxBackground" useParentLevel="true" inherits="TooltipBackdropTemplate">
				<KeyValues>
					<KeyValue key="backdropBorderColor" value="DARKGRAY_COLOR" type="global"/>
					<KeyValue key="backdropColor" value="BLACK_FONT_COLOR" type="global"/>
				</KeyValues>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" x="4" y="-85"/>
					<Anchor point="TOPRIGHT" x="-31" y="-85"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" x="4" y="50"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="-31" y="50"/>
				</Anchors>
			</Frame>

			<!--
				**Declared after the inset, because it hangs off it.** An anchor pointing at a
				sibling that does not exist yet is dropped without a word
				(`tools/check-xml-anchors.js`).
			-->
			<Button parentKey="ScrollBox" inherits="WowScrollBoxList" frameLevel="3">
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="TOPLEFT" x="5" y="-5"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="-5" y="5"/>
				</Anchors>
				<Layers>
					<Layer level="ARTWORK">
						<FontString parentKey="EmptyText" hidden="true" inherits="GameFontDisable" justifyH="CENTER">
							<Size x="320" y="0"/>
							<Anchors>
								<Anchor point="TOP" x="0" y="-40"/>
							</Anchors>
						</FontString>
					</Layer>
				</Layers>
			</Button>
			<EventFrame parentKey="ScrollBar" inherits="MinimalScrollBar">
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT" x="5" y="-5"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="5" y="5"/>
				</Anchors>
			</EventFrame>

			<!--
				**The one thing this tab does that is not on a row**, and since 3c the only visible
				way to make a switch at all: the portrait's dropdown that used to carry it is gone
				(§6-C). The empty-list text points here.

				Same place and same size as the export panel's own button, on the frame's bottom
				edge rather than under the inset, which is where the click binding window puts one
				(`Blizzard_ClickBindingUI.xml`). Text is set in `OnLoad`: `text=` on the template
				takes a global's name, and ours live in `L`.

				**Not disabled in combat, unlike the toggles on the rows.** Those press a value the
				restricted side has to be told about; this writes a definition nothing is bound to
				yet, and the rebuild behind it stands itself down until the fight ends
				(`UpdateBindings`). Greying it would be a lockout with nothing behind it.
			-->
			<Button parentKey="NewButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate">
				<Size x="140" y="23"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="-6" y="5"/>
				</Anchors>
				<Scripts>
					<OnClick>
						self:GetParent():OnNewClick();
					</OnClick>
				</Scripts>
			</Button>
		</Frames>
		<Scripts>
			<OnLoad method="OnLoad"/>
			<OnShow method="OnShow"/>
			<OnHide method="OnHide"/>
			<OnEvent method="OnEvent"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Frame>
</Ui>
